---
import UniPage from '../components/UniPage.astro';
import { getUniversity } from '../data/universities';

const uni = getUniversity('mumbai')!;
---

<UniPage
  uni={uni}
  title="Mumbai University CGPA to Percentage Calculator (Pre-2026 & Post-2026) | GlobalCGPA"
  description="Convert Mumbai University CGPA to percentage. Pre-2026: (7.1×CGPA)+11 (general) or (7.4×CGPA)+12 (engineering). Post-2026: raw marks-based conversion per Circular 803/2026. Free calculator, conversion table, class thresholds and FAQ."
  intro="Enter your CGPA to convert using the correct rule for your transcript date. Pre-2026 transcripts use the formula-based conversion; post-2026 transcripts use raw marks with opt-in certificate."
>
  <h2>Important: Rule Change Effective 1 January 2026</h2>
  <p><strong>Mumbai University repealed all formula-based CGPA-to-percentage conversion</strong> via <strong>Circular No. Exam/Result/803 of 2026</strong>, dated 1 January 2026. The change is forward-looking and applies based on your transcript date:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Transcripts dated on or before 31 December 2025 (pre-2026):</strong> Use the pre-2026 formulas below.</li>
    <li><strong>Transcripts dated 1 January 2026 or later (post-2026):</strong> No formula applies. The college computes percentage from actual raw marks (total obtained ÷ total maximum × 100) and issues a conversion certificate only on student request.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The calculator below applies the pre-2026 formulas for reference. If your transcript is post-2026, you must request a conversion certificate from your college — the percentage will be based on your actual semester marks, not a formula.</p>

  <h2>Pre-2026 Formulas (Transcripts ≤ 31 Dec 2025)</h2>
  <p>Under the now-repealed Circular Exam/Com/97 of 2018 (re-affirmed by Exam/Result/157 of 2019), the official formulas were:</p>
  <div class="formula-block">
    <div class="f-label">Pre-2026: General programmes (CBCGS/CBSGS)</div>
    <div class="f-text">Percentage = (7.1 × CGPA) + 11</div>
    <div class="f-example">Example: 8.0 CGPA → (7.1 × 8) + 11 = <strong>67.8%</strong></div>
  </div>
  <div class="formula-block">
    <div class="f-label">Pre-2026: Engineering (Faculty of Technology, CGPA ≥ 7)</div>
    <div class="f-text">Percentage = (7.4 × CGPA) + 12</div>
    <div class="f-example">Example: 8.0 CGPA → (7.4 × 8) + 12 = <strong>71.2%</strong></div>
  </div>
  <div class="formula-block">
    <div class="f-label">Pre-2026: Engineering (Faculty of Technology, CGPA < 7)</div>
    <div class="f-text">Percentage = (7.1 × CGPA) + 11.6</div>
    <div class="f-example">Example: 6.5 CGPA → (7.1 × 6.5) + 11.6 = <strong>57.75%</strong></div>
  </div>
  <p>The calculator above uses the general formula. For engineering, select the appropriate variant if you know your faculty and CGPA range.</p>

  <h3>Worked Examples (Pre-2026 General Formula)</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>CGPA</th><th>Calculation</th><th>MU Percentage</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>9.5</td><td>(7.1 × 9.5) + 11</td><td>78.5%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>9.0</td><td>(7.1 × 9.0) + 11</td><td>74.9%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>8.0</td><td>(7.1 × 8.0) + 11</td><td>67.8%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7.0</td><td>(7.1 × 7.0) + 11</td><td>60.7%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6.0</td><td>(7.1 × 6.0) + 11</td><td>53.6%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Post-2026: No Formula — Raw Marks Conversion (Transcripts ≥ 1 Jan 2026)</h2>
  <p>Under <strong>Circular No. Exam/Result/803 of 2026</strong>, effective 1 January 2026:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The formula-based conversion is <strong>repealed</strong>. The earlier circulars (Exam/Com/97 of 2018 and Exam/Result/157 of 2019) are explicitly listed as repealed.</li>
    <li>There is <strong>no replacement formula</strong>. The university no longer uses a multiplier or offset.</li>
    <li>Percentage is computed from <strong>actual raw marks</strong>: total marks obtained across all semesters (Sem I–VI) divided by total maximum marks across those semesters, × 100.</li>
    <li>The CGPA itself does not enter the calculation.</li>
    <li>Conversion is <strong>opt-in</strong>: the percentage is not printed on the transcript by default. You must request a separate conversion certificate from your affiliated college (which forwards to the university for attestation if needed).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>This means a student with 9.6 CGPA whose actual marks average 88.2% will receive 88.2% on a 2026 conversion certificate — not the 79.2% the old formula would have produced.</p>

  <h2>Why Your MU Percentage Looks Different from Other Universities</h2>
  <p>The pre-2026 formula (7.1×CGPA + 11) compresses the scale: a 10.0 CGPA converts to 82%, not 100%. Each 1.0 CGPA step is worth only 7.1 percentage points. This is a calibration difference, not a reflection of your performance. Recruiters familiar with Mumbai University use the same rule, so the numbers align once everyone applies the correct formula for the transcript date.</p>
  <p><strong>Never use the ×9.5 or ×10 formulas for Mumbai University.</strong> An 8.0 CGPA written as 80% instead of the official 67.8% (pre-2026) creates a mismatch with your documents that can get an application rejected during verification.</p>

  <h2>Class Thresholds (Pre-2026)</h2>
  <p>Under the pre-2026 formula, <strong>First Class</strong> begins around 6.48 CGPA (57%), and the pass boundary sits near 4.0 CGPA. Because the offset compresses the scale, small CGPA improvements move your percentage more slowly than at other universities — going from 7.0 to 8.0 CGPA adds only about 7 percentage points.</p>
  <p>If your target is a 60% eligibility cut-off for a company or government form, you need roughly a 6.9 CGPA at Mumbai University (pre-2026) — noticeably higher than the 6.0 that would clear the same bar under VTU's ×10 rule.</p>

  <h2>Official Documents and Certificates</h2>
  <p><strong>Pre-2026 transcripts:</strong> Your MU consolidated marksheet and degree certificate state your CGPA and the converted percentage (using the old formula). If an employer or university insists on an official percentage document, request a conversion certificate from your college's examination cell.</p>
  <p><strong>Post-2026 transcripts:</strong> The consolidated marksheet carries only your CGPA. For a percentage, you must request a conversion certificate from your college's examination cell, citing Circular Exam/Result/803 of 2026. The college will compute the percentage from your raw semester marks and issue the certificate. Turnaround is typically 7–21 working days.</p>
  <p>For semester-wise percentage breakdowns (e.g., final-year percentage for a form), convert each semester's SGPA individually or use our <a href="/sgpa-to-cgpa">SGPA to CGPA calculator</a> first.</p>
</UniPage>
